<p>
  A diode conducts current in one direction and blocks it in the other — and testing whether it still does that correctly is one of the fastest fault-finding checks you can run with a multimeter. Most digital multimeters have a dedicated diode test mode for exactly this purpose. This guide covers how to use it, what a good reading looks like, and how to catch the three common diode failure modes.
</p>

<h2 id="diode-test-mode">Using the Diode Test Function</h2>

<p>
  Set the multimeter's rotary dial to the diode test symbol (a diode symbol with an arrow, often sharing a position with continuity test). In this mode, the meter applies a small test current through its probes and displays the forward voltage drop across whatever is connected — unlike resistance mode, which is not designed to characterise a semiconductor junction correctly.
</p>

<ol>
  <li>Ensure the circuit is fully powered off and, ideally, the diode is isolated from the circuit — a diode still wired into a circuit can be influenced by parallel paths and give a misleading reading.</li>
  <li>Connect the red probe to the diode's anode and the black probe to the cathode (the cathode is marked with a printed band on the diode body).</li>
  <li>Read the display: a healthy silicon diode shows a forward voltage drop, typically in the 0.5–0.8 V range.</li>
  <li>Reverse the probes. A healthy diode should now show "OL" (open loop/overrange) — no conduction in the reverse direction.</li>
</ol>

