<Callout type="tip" title="LEDs sometimes glow faintly in diode test mode">
  The small test current the meter applies is often enough to make an LED glow dimly — this is normal and a handy way to visually confirm an LED's polarity and function on the bench.
</Callout>

<h2 id="fault-modes">The Three Diode Failure Modes</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Shorted diode</strong>: Reads a low voltage drop (often close to 0 V) in <em>both</em> directions. The junction has broken down and the diode now conducts both ways — a shorted diode is a common cause of a blown fuse or dead-short symptom elsewhere in the circuit.</li>
  <li><strong>Open diode</strong>: Reads "OL" in <em>both</em> directions. The junction is broken and the diode no longer conducts at all, even forward-biased.</li>
  <li><strong>Leaky diode</strong>: Shows the correct forward reading, but the reverse direction shows some finite voltage/conduction instead of a clean "OL" — the diode still functions but with degraded reverse-blocking performance, which can cause intermittent or load-dependent circuit faults that are harder to diagnose than a clean short or open.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="in-circuit-caution">Testing a Diode In-Circuit</h2>

<p>
  A diode tested while still connected in a circuit can show a false reading if other components — resistors, other diodes, transformer windings — provide a parallel current path around it. If an in-circuit reading looks ambiguous or doesn't match the expected values above, desolder at least one leg of the diode and retest in isolation before concluding it has failed.
</p>

